WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.670
Welcome to Investigate IQ, an investigation copilot for
reviewing banking alerts.

2
00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:13.810
We will follow Case zero four one from its alert through
transactions, a cited draft, evidence retrieval, and a human
next step.

3
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:22.535
We will then open a separate fictional intake packet to show
how a new numeric case is actually stored and recalculated.

4
00:00:22.535 --> 00:00:24.612
Both paths use fabricated data.

5
00:00:24.612 --> 00:00:30.429
Neither determines whether money is legal or illegal, and
neither files a regulatory report.

6
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.843
We begin on Home.

7
00:00:33.843 --> 00:00:39.506
Enter a display name, here Case Reviewer, and choose the
Investigator workflow role.

8
00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:41.249
Then open the workspace.

9
00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:48.220
The name helps attribute actions inside Investigate IQ; it is
not bank-grade login or identity verification.

10
00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:56.933
Later, if a decision is submitted, the product requires a
written rationale and saves the action to its audit history.

11
00:00:56.933 --> 00:01:00.855
Selecting a role alone does not make a decision.

12
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.763
Here is the route we will actually follow.

13
00:01:06.763 --> 00:01:10.425
A fictional monitoring alert enters the Case Queue.

14
00:01:10.425 --> 00:01:16.377
The investigator opens the case, checks source transactions,
and reads the copilot's draft.

15
00:01:16.377 --> 00:01:24.618
Evidence and R A G shows exactly which synthetic playbook
document was indexed and what chunks were retrieved.

16
00:01:24.618 --> 00:01:31.027
The investigator, not the AI, chooses whether to close, ask
for information, or escalate.

17
00:01:31.027 --> 00:01:33.774
Only a submitted escalation enters Compliance.

18
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:42.161
Home explains what this product does and gives a route into
the Case Queue.

19
00:01:42.161 --> 00:01:52.550
The left navigation also includes Investigation Workspace,
Evidence and R A G, Compliance Queue, Analytics, Global
Search, an information page, and two admin screens.

20
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:59.477
The floating Ask Investigate IQ launcher stays at the lower
right, independent of the navigation menu.

21
00:01:59.477 --> 00:02:04.671
Opening it does not change the case or leave the current page.

22
00:02:04.671 --> 00:02:07.269
We will visit each operational screen.

23
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.505
The Case Queue is where an investigator begins.

24
00:02:12.505 --> 00:02:17.613
It lists fictional alerts with severity, status, scenario, and
recorded trigger-rule metadata.

25
00:02:17.613 --> 00:02:24.423
The counts and filters help find work, but an alert is not
evidence of a crime.

26
00:02:24.423 --> 00:02:28.679
This is not connected to a live bank monitoring system.

27
00:02:28.679 --> 00:02:35.915
The example we will use is Case zero four one, Coastal
Wholesale Traders, a high-severity structuring-style alert.

28
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.707
Type Coastal into the customer filter.

29
00:02:40.707 --> 00:02:44.184
The list narrows to Case zero four one.

30
00:02:44.184 --> 00:02:52.005
Its row shows the customer, the structuring alert label, high
severity, open status, and recorded rule R three.

31
00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:57.654
The clearly labeled Open button takes us directly to this
case's Investigation Workspace.

32
00:02:57.654 --> 00:03:03.738
The rule label is source-dataset metadata; this screen does
not independently recalculate the alert.

33
00:03:03.738 --> 00:03:07.649
We open the case to check what actually happened.

34
00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:16.760
The workspace keeps the selected case and offers a saved
investigation replay or live analysis.

35
00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:22.533
For a reliable walkthrough, we choose the saved Case zero four
one report.

36
00:03:22.533 --> 00:03:27.861
It is a previously generated AI draft, not a fresh model run.

37
00:03:27.861 --> 00:03:32.745
Live analysis is available only when a model connection is
configured.

38
00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:42.958
The pipeline gathers alert details, customer K Y C,
transaction patterns, relationships, available documents, and
playbook guidance before drafting findings for human review.

39
00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:49.598
The customer is Coastal Wholesale Traders, a wholesale
distribution business.

40
00:03:49.598 --> 00:03:55.896
The review window contains six credits from September twenty
second through September twenty seventh.

41
00:03:55.896 --> 00:04:01.294
The account's prior baseline average is three hundred ninety
five thousand rupees.

42
00:04:01.294 --> 00:04:10.741
The trigger transaction is nine hundred seventy five thousand
rupees, which is displayed as about two point five times that
baseline.

43
00:04:10.741 --> 00:04:14.340
There are no prior cases in this dataset.

44
00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.388
These are context facts to verify, not a verdict.

45
00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.870
The six credits are not all identical.

46
00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:41.465
Four are recorded as cash deposits: transaction nineteen nine
two seven for nine hundred seventy five thousand, nineteen
nine two eight for nine hundred sixty thousand, nineteen nine
three zero for nine hundred fifty five thousand, and nineteen
nine three two for nine hundred eighty thousand rupees.

47
00:04:41.465 --> 00:04:46.608
Together those four cash entries total three million eight
hundred seventy thousand rupees.

48
00:04:46.608 --> 00:04:55.708
The other two credits are recorded as payments from Retail
Partner A and B, so they should not silently be described as
cash.

49
00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:04.594
The draft marks the deposit cluster as a red flag and cites
the four cash transaction IDs.

50
00:05:04.594 --> 00:05:10.706
A separate finding says the file lacks documentation
explaining why this pattern fits the customer's business.

51
00:05:10.706 --> 00:05:17.200
That missing document is not proof of an improper purpose; it
is a reason to ask questions.

52
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.930
The report asks for the business rationale and the agreements
with the two retail partners.

53
00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:30.188
The Grounding Validator checks selected IDs and numeric
wording, but not every sentence or the truth of an
explanation.

54
00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.924
The floating Copilot opens on the right and shows the selected
case.

55
00:05:36.924 --> 00:05:45.366
Suggested questions can fill the message field, and the
investigator can also type a question and press Send at the
bottom.

56
00:05:45.366 --> 00:05:47.778
The conversation appears above the composer.

57
00:05:47.778 --> 00:05:52.602
The selected case's answer can point to stored transactions or
indexed passages.

58
00:05:52.602 --> 00:05:57.828
The saved investigation report is a separate historical draft,
not a second chat.

59
00:05:57.828 --> 00:06:03.054
The Copilot's current answer is bounded by the records it can
actually read.

60
00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:09.502
Notice the distinction between a plausible answer and a
verified conclusion.

61
00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:13.904
A citation tells us which transaction or playbook entry to
inspect.

62
00:06:13.904 --> 00:06:19.506
It does not prove the prose is correct or that the activity is
unlawful.

63
00:06:19.506 --> 00:06:28.710
The saved report even proposes escalation, but our second
playbook requires a documented request for an explanation and
no reasonable answer on file.

64
00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:32.312
We will not let the draft skip that condition.

65
00:06:32.312 --> 00:06:35.513
The investigator retains ownership of the next step.

66
00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:44.761
Now ask the case Copilot about transactions that are stored
and whether K Y C is independently verified.

67
00:06:44.761 --> 00:06:51.571
For Case zero four one, the current answer counts sixteen rows
in the supplied account dataset.

68
00:06:51.571 --> 00:06:56.679
The older saved report captured six transactions in its
narrower review window.

69
00:06:56.679 --> 00:06:59.658
Neither number is a complete bank-month statement.

70
00:06:59.658 --> 00:07:09.022
The source alert identifies transaction nineteen nine two
seven as its trigger; it does not independently classify every
other row as suspicious.

71
00:07:09.022 --> 00:07:18.386
The workbook K Y C field says Verified, but zero original
identity files were uploaded, so that label is not independent
verification.

72
00:07:18.386 --> 00:07:21.791
The answer cites stored case passages for inspection.

73
00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:26.554
A second question tests evidence provenance.

74
00:07:26.554 --> 00:07:33.917
The historical saved report captured zero case passages, while
the current index contains twenty one for this case.

75
00:07:33.917 --> 00:07:38.007
The copilot names both scopes and their recorded times
separately.

76
00:07:38.007 --> 00:07:44.552
It does not claim to know the old index version or when the
extra passages arrived.

77
00:07:44.552 --> 00:07:52.324
A current citation cannot retroactively prove what the old
report saw; the old report is retained as a snapshot.

78
00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:58.932
Follow the copilot's cited case-ledger chunk to Evidence and R
A G.

79
00:07:58.932 --> 00:08:03.764
The source identifier, verification status, and exact stored
passage are visible here.

80
00:08:03.764 --> 00:08:11.414
This first chunk warns that the account rows are only the
supplied fictional dataset, not a complete bank statement.

81
00:08:11.414 --> 00:08:19.065
The citation is an inspectable pointer; it does not
authenticate the source or prove what happened at another
bank.

82
00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:26.959
The investigator can also ask the floating, page-aware
Copilot, What does this chunk say?

83
00:08:26.959 --> 00:08:30.726
The page shows passage C zero beside the answer.

84
00:08:30.726 --> 00:08:37.004
The answer quotes the exact stored text and identifies its
status as source rows only.

85
00:08:37.004 --> 00:08:42.026
It warns that the supplied dataset is not a complete bank
statement.

86
00:08:42.026 --> 00:08:48.304
It does not invent an original document, counterparty K Y C,
or a legal-money verdict.

87
00:08:48.304 --> 00:08:52.489
This is the boundary between useful retrieval and unsupported
claims.

88
00:08:54.100 --> 00:09:00.366
From the cited playbook, we move to Evidence and R A G without
leaving the site.

89
00:09:00.366 --> 00:09:06.240
For this case, we use Source and chunks, Search the index, and
Method and code.

90
00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:12.114
The page also offers Case records and a Rule Lab; Fictional
Intake appears when enabled.

91
00:09:12.114 --> 00:09:17.205
The playbook collection has twenty four synthetic documents
and fifty seven indexed chunks.

92
00:09:17.205 --> 00:09:23.863
Case zero four one points to playbook P B A M L S T R zero
one.

93
00:09:23.863 --> 00:09:28.954
A document citation identifies a source; it does not prove
every generated claim.

94
00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:40.321
Here is the original text of P B A M L S T R zero one, titled
Identify and document a structuring pattern.

95
00:09:40.321 --> 00:09:46.335
It instructs the investigator to list exact transactions,
amounts, dates, channels, and the combined value.

96
00:09:46.335 --> 00:09:51.947
It also asks whether amounts are similar and whether the
channel or counterparty changes.

97
00:09:51.947 --> 00:09:57.961
We can see the source itself, rather than trusting only an AI
summary of it.

98
00:09:57.961 --> 00:10:03.173
This is synthetic guidance, not an approved Indian-bank policy
or an official regulation.

99
00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:10.396
The builder stores one metadata chunk containing the document
title and escalation criterion.

100
00:10:10.396 --> 00:10:16.507
It then splits the body at sentence-ending punctuation and
groups two sentences per body chunk.

101
00:10:16.507 --> 00:10:21.803
This source produces three indexed chunks: C zero, C one, and
C two.

102
00:10:21.803 --> 00:10:28.728
The selected investigation retrieved the metadata chunk C
zero, while the body chunks preserve the detailed
instructions.

103
00:10:28.728 --> 00:10:36.468
Each passage can be opened and read on the same site, with its
source ID and word count visible.

104
00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:43.594
The Search the index view lets us type terms such as
structuring cash deposits.

105
00:10:43.594 --> 00:10:51.834
SQLite F T S five matches those terms against the stored
chunks, and B M twenty five ranks the matching passages.

106
00:10:51.834 --> 00:10:59.682
The Evidence Agent uses this search with the alert scenario as
a filter, so the case receives relevant playbook guidance.

107
00:10:59.682 --> 00:11:03.998
This is lexical keyword retrieval, not embeddings or a vector
database.

108
00:11:03.998 --> 00:11:08.314
Search results can open their source chunk here on the site.

109
00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:13.874
This is the full document path behind the screen.

110
00:11:13.874 --> 00:11:18.068
The authored markdown playbook is parsed into a source record.

111
00:11:18.068 --> 00:11:22.261
The builder creates a metadata chunk and two-sentence body
chunks.

112
00:11:22.261 --> 00:11:25.616
SQLite stores those chunks and indexes their words.

113
00:11:25.616 --> 00:11:29.390
For a case, the Evidence Agent retrieves scenario-matched
passages.

114
00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:38.196
The draft can cite a playbook ID, and the Grounding Validator
checks that the ID actually came from the retrieved set.

115
00:11:38.196 --> 00:11:43.228
Finally, the human opens the source and judges whether the
guidance applies.

116
00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:50.406
The Method and code view keeps the implementation inside
Investigate IQ.

117
00:11:50.406 --> 00:12:01.500
It has eight expandable steps covering alert calculation,
guidance and case chunking, case and guidance search,
transaction links, context gathering, and citation checks.

118
00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:04.394
This diagram highlights four representative functions.

119
00:12:04.394 --> 00:12:16.453
The code supports explainability, but the validator is
limited: it checks selected citations and wording patterns,
not the full factual correctness of a generated report.

120
00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:27.364
Back in the Investigation Workspace, the Human Decision panel
offers close with no concern, request more information, or
escalate to Compliance.

121
00:12:27.364 --> 00:12:31.335
The AI cannot select or submit any of them.

122
00:12:31.335 --> 00:12:36.629
The investigator's name and written reason are required for a
recorded decision.

123
00:12:36.629 --> 00:12:44.569
This matters because the retrieved playbook is conditional,
and the case file has no documented business explanation yet.

124
00:12:44.569 --> 00:12:49.863
A machine-generated escalation suggestion cannot replace those
checks or the human decision.

125
00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:57.564
For this example, Request more information is the cautious
option shown in the form.

126
00:12:57.564 --> 00:13:06.932
The proposed rationale asks for the business explanation of
the cash-deposit cluster and agreements with Retail Partner A
and B before a final conclusion.

127
00:13:06.932 --> 00:13:13.567
That is not the same as declaring the activity harmless; it
keeps the issue open for follow-up.

128
00:13:13.567 --> 00:13:15.519
The rationale is drafted here.

129
00:13:15.519 --> 00:13:22.154
Until Submit decision is pressed, no decision is recorded and
the case does not move to Compliance.

130
00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:26.568
Now we open the Compliance Queue.

131
00:13:26.568 --> 00:13:33.561
The Case zero four one form was not submitted, so that example
did not create an escalation.

132
00:13:33.561 --> 00:13:41.377
The case shown here is a separate fictional transaction packet
that an investigator explicitly escalated with a written
reason.

133
00:13:41.377 --> 00:13:45.079
We will inspect how that packet was created shortly.

134
00:13:45.079 --> 00:13:52.072
Compliance can review the reason, acknowledge the case, return
it for information, or record a referral decision.

135
00:13:52.072 --> 00:13:58.243
The product does not automatically file a suspicious
transaction report or communicate with a regulator.

136
00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:05.116
Analytics summarizes the fictional alert set by severity,
scenario, status, and time.

137
00:14:05.116 --> 00:14:15.147
It is a workload and exploration view: users can see how many
alerts are open or resolved and which patterns occur in the
sample.

138
00:14:15.147 --> 00:14:20.162
It does not measure real-world crime detection, false
positives, or model accuracy.

139
00:14:20.162 --> 00:14:26.849
Those claims would need independent evaluation and real bank
data, which Investigate IQ does not have.

140
00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:31.884
Global Search is the quickest cross-reference tool.

141
00:14:31.884 --> 00:14:38.645
Search Coastal and the site locates the customer, related
case, account, and transactions in the fictional dataset.

142
00:14:38.645 --> 00:14:46.599
A result can take us back to the Investigation Workspace, so
we do not have to remember a case number.

143
00:14:46.599 --> 00:14:55.349
Evidence and R A G is different: it lets us inspect source
documents and search guidance or case-evidence chunks within
their scope.

144
00:14:55.349 --> 00:14:58.929
Search is a pointer to records, not a conclusion.

145
00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:05.449
This information page identifies Investigate IQ and explains
its purpose.

146
00:15:05.449 --> 00:15:14.147
It is separate from the investigation workflow: opening it
does not change a case, its evidence, or a recorded decision.

147
00:15:14.147 --> 00:15:17.191
We now return to the operational screens.

148
00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:21.182
Now open Admin Knowledge Base.

149
00:15:21.182 --> 00:15:28.679
Enter the configured passcode in the masked field and select
Unlock; we never display or speak the code.

150
00:15:28.679 --> 00:15:36.176
The passcode opens an inspector, but it is only a limited
gate, not bank-grade identity or role authorization.

151
00:15:36.176 --> 00:15:42.840
After unlocking, we can inspect the actual source documents,
their chunks, and a live retrieval query.

152
00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:53.593
The unlocked page reports twenty four synthetic playbook
documents, fifty seven indexed chunks, and four hundred
seventy six searchable terms.

153
00:15:53.593 --> 00:15:57.415
This is the guidance corpus for the source-database copilot.

154
00:15:57.415 --> 00:16:04.210
It is separate from customer K Y C records and from the new
fictional intake packet.

155
00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:10.155
Search here is lexical full-text retrieval, not an embedding
model or a vector database.

156
00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:20.054
Open playbook P B A M L S T R zero one, the structuring
guidance connected to Case zero four one.

157
00:16:20.054 --> 00:16:23.463
Its complete source text appears above the chunk table.

158
00:16:23.463 --> 00:16:31.418
One metadata chunk stores its title and escalation criterion;
the body is split at sentence boundaries and grouped in two-
sentence passages.

159
00:16:31.418 --> 00:16:38.614
Here we can see C zero, C one, and C two with their exact
source text and word counts.

160
00:16:38.614 --> 00:16:43.539
A chunk is a searchable excerpt, not proof that a transaction
is criminal.

161
00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:48.867
Type structuring cash deposits into the live retrieval field.

162
00:16:48.867 --> 00:16:56.400
SQLite F T S five matches the indexed words and B M twenty
five ranks the returned passages.

163
00:16:56.400 --> 00:17:07.281
The first result here is a conditional escalation passage: the
pattern, implausible business fit, and no reasonable
explanation after a documented request must all be examined.

164
00:17:07.281 --> 00:17:16.488
The copilot can cite a source identifier, but the investigator
still reads this exact passage and compares it with the
transaction records.

165
00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:22.207
The second admin page has its own masked passcode step.

166
00:17:22.207 --> 00:17:27.135
After unlocking, it displays saved R one and R two preview
settings.

167
00:17:27.135 --> 00:17:34.939
These controls do not constitute the bank's production
monitoring engine, and changing them does not regenerate
existing queue alerts.

168
00:17:34.939 --> 00:17:41.510
We will inspect the saved values and run a read-only preview
without saving a new configuration.

169
00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:53.279
The saved example settings are five times the account baseline
for R one, eighty percent outbound proxy for R two, and a
seventy two hour observation window.

170
00:17:53.279 --> 00:18:02.704
R two compares aggregate debits from the same account; it does
not prove that the same credited rupees funded those debits or
crossed another bank.

171
00:18:02.704 --> 00:18:08.360
Sliders allow a proposed change, but nothing is written until
a named admin presses Save.

172
00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:14.797
At the current settings, the read-only preview checked three
hundred fictional accounts.

173
00:18:14.797 --> 00:18:18.320
Two met R one; none met both preview conditions.

174
00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:28.106
This is a sensitivity check over a sample, not the count of
live alerts and not the calculation used for the separate
fictional intake case.

175
00:18:28.106 --> 00:18:32.020
We do not save any threshold change in this walkthrough.

176
00:18:34.100 --> 00:18:38.252
Now open Fictional Intake inside Evidence and R A G.

177
00:18:38.252 --> 00:18:49.045
Enter an asserted monthly baseline of eighty thousand rupees,
an incoming transfer amount of three hundred thousand, and an
outgoing amount of one hundred eighty thousand.

178
00:18:49.045 --> 00:19:03.161
The application creates ten labeled synthetic rows, calculates
a review signal, and saves one case in SQLite with five
generated text records: profile, invalid sample P A N, invalid
sample passport, ledger, and alert.

179
00:19:03.161 --> 00:19:06.482
No bank document or third-party alert is uploaded.

180
00:19:06.482 --> 00:19:09.803
The case then appears in the Case Queue.

181
00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:16.456
Open the stored ledger rows rather than trusting a static
card.

182
00:19:16.456 --> 00:19:22.000
Four completed credits of three hundred thousand total one
million two hundred thousand rupees.

183
00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:27.148
Six completed debits of one hundred eighty thousand total one
million eighty thousand.

184
00:19:27.148 --> 00:19:34.672
The recorded external counterparty identifiers tell us where
this fictional bank's ledger says money came from or went to.

185
00:19:34.672 --> 00:19:42.988
They do not supply the other bank's customer K Y C, opposite-
side posting, or a verified chain of the same rupees.

186
00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:50.064
In the saved packet, each generated record can be read in
full.

187
00:19:50.064 --> 00:19:54.615
Here the exact indexed passages and the case-scoped search are
visible.

188
00:19:54.615 --> 00:20:04.543
The source text is split into passages, given a S H A two five
six digest, and searched with SQLite F T S five.

189
00:20:04.543 --> 00:20:09.507
The result shows the saved transaction text, not a model's
invented answer.

190
00:20:09.507 --> 00:20:19.435
But identity samples are deliberately invalid, no original
identity file was uploaded, counterparty K Y C is unknown, and
independent source-of-funds proof is missing.

191
00:20:19.435 --> 00:20:25.227
A transfer chain and a citation cannot establish whether money
is legal or illegal.

192
00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:30.587
Open the new case in the Investigation Workspace.

193
00:20:30.587 --> 00:20:37.125
Its separate calculated review reloads the saved rows and
checks source fingerprints and exact chunks.

194
00:20:37.125 --> 00:20:49.330
The observed amounts are one million two hundred thousand in
and one million eighty thousand out: fifteen times the
asserted monthly baseline, ninety percent outbound, and six
beneficiaries.

195
00:20:49.330 --> 00:20:58.920
